<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<Pathway xmlns="http://pathvisio.org/GPML/2021" title="coenzyme A biosynthesis I (bacteria)" organism="cellular organisms" source="PlantCyc" version="20260304101735">
  <Xref identifier="COA-PWY" dataSource="MetaCyc" />
  <Description>&lt;i&gt;Supporting evidence for this pathway in Oryza sativa Japonica Group&lt;/i&gt;: This pathway is on the list of Common Viridiplantae Pathways (CVP) generated for the COMET project, which includes pathways that are thought to be present in all or most Viridiplantae species including algae. All UPP pathways are automatically imported into databases regardless of Pathologic predictions. &lt;A href=&quot;http://www.plantcyc.org/about/savi-pipeline&quot;&gt;[more info]&lt;/A&gt; &lt;i&gt;Summary from MetaCyc:&lt;/i&gt; &lt;b&gt;General Background&lt;/b&gt; ||FRAME: CO-A &quot;Coenzyme A&quot;| (CoA) is a ubiquitous compound found in archaea, bacteria, plants, and animals. It is a common acyl carrier in prokaryotic and eukaryotic cells, and is required for a multitude of reactions for both biosynthetic and degradative pathways, including the oxidation of fatty acids, carbohydrates, and amino acids |CITS: [16415216]|. The biosynthesis of CoA occurs via different pathways in archaea, bacteria, and eukaryotes, and thus enzymes specific for the bacterial pathway are targets for antibacterial drug discovery. &lt;b&gt;About This Pathway&lt;/b&gt; The first compound in this pathway, |FRAME: 4-P-PANTOTHENATE|, is produced by phosphorylation of |FRAME: PANTOTHENATE|, as described in |FRAME: PANTO-PWY|. Only microorganisms and plants (including some eukaryotic microbes, such as |FRAME: TAX-4932|) can synthesize |FRAME: PANTOTHENATE| &lt;i&gt;de novo&lt;/i&gt; - animals must obtain it in their diet, giving it the name vitamin B&lt;sub&gt;5&lt;/sub&gt;. The first step in this pathway is the ligation of |FRAME: 4-P-PANTOTHENATE| with |FRAME: CYS|. Prokaryotic organisms utilize a CTP dependent enzyme - |FRAME: EC-6.3.2.5|. This is the only difference between the prokaryotic pathway and the eukaryotic pathway (see |FRAME: PWY-7851|), since eukaryotes utilize an ATP-dependent enzyme (|FRAME: EC-6.3.2.51|). The pathway continues by decarboxylation to |FRAME: PANTETHEINE-P|, which is catalyzed by |FRAME: EC-4.1.1.36|. The next step consists of the transfer of an adenyly group from ATP to |FRAME: PANTETHEINE-P|, forming |FRAME: DEPHOSPHO-COA|. The ultimate reaction is a phosphorylation catalyzed by |FRAME: EC-2.7.1.24|, producing |FRAME: CO-A|. All of the enzymes of this pathway are essential for growth.</Description>
  <Authors>
    <Author name="arnaud" />
  </Authors>
